00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:33.740
on speed.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.060
On the street, as a delivery worker, you know, you're mostly alone.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:39.960
You're moving fast from place to place.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:42.240
And you spend a lot of time basically in your own head.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:45.750
And some people, you know, play little games on the job.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.340
They set little challenges for themselves.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:59.040
And just like there's a euphoria for the people who are under so much pressure at Federal Express in the middle of the night, there's this euphoria to moving through traffic and racing the clock to get your stuff where it's supposed to go.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:02.200
Tony Starbucks delivered delivered produce in San Francisco.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:08.740
At his job, he used to wake up at 2.30 every morning, get stoned on the loading dock with the other drivers, and head out.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:14.400
Everybody had their own routes, and there were, you know, sort of like expected times that you would finish your routes in.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:22.160
And part of the reasons why we had so many accidents, especially me, was because I was obsessed with doing my route faster than anybody else.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:27.740
And I would literally, like, shave, like, I think I got it when I first started the route, I shaved, like, an hour off of, you know.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:30.370
So instead of doing it in three hours, I would do it in two hours.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:41.400
But along the way, you know, I would wipe out people's cars, I wouldn't talk to the clients, I would drop off the potatoes, and they would try to engage me in a conversation, and all I could think about is, let me just get the hell out of here and, like, you know, get on my way.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:52.060
The way I would shave the time is I ran a lot of red lights.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.840
I would drive 65 miles an hour down a city street.

00:06:56.150 --> 00:07:06.740
It got to the point, though, where I realized that after, whatever, a year of driving, that I was getting into increments of, like, one minute, 30 seconds.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:13.820
I mean, literally, I would, like, drive 100 miles an hour into the loading bay, slam on the brakes, and look at the clock.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:42.960
I remember one day driving along 50 miles an hour down this street, and I see it's a two-lane, one-way street, and I see in the right-hand lane about two blocks ahead of me, like, just your nondescript white rental car just sort of going along and, like, can't decide if they want to go right or left.

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:52.300
And two blocks away from them, I go, you know, if I really wanted to, I could slow down right now because I know they're going to do something really stupid.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:54.640
But then there was this part of me that said, well, to hell with that.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:56.680
I'm just, I'm on my route, I have to do what I have to do.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:02.180
And of course, as I got within like 20 feet of them, they decided to turn left from the right lane, right in front of me.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:08.400
I completely totaled their car, and my first reaction was to get out of the car, and I wanted to yell at them.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:16.040
It wasn't like, you know, you could have prevented this, you saw this coming, but it was like, you have now slowed me down, you've screwed up my whole day.

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:29.900
And really, if it wasn't for this group of PG&amp;E workers, electrical workers working on the street out there, who saw this and somehow decided that I was in the right, because I guess it was like this blue-collar bonding thing.

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:31.220
Like, he's a truck driver.

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:34.840
There's no way these people in this rented car, you know, can be right.

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:39.420
I think I would have really gotten into trouble because I obviously was speeding like a maniac.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:44.780
So, you know, I sort of like walked through my whole job that way, except the time I ran into the Bank of America building.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:52.800
You're in this very surreal world.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:54.220
I mean, nobody else is up.

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:56.760
You're bombing down the streets.

00:08:57.610 --> 00:08:58.930
I think that's part of the problem.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:04.480
When all of a sudden people appear on the streets, you're like, look, I've been on the streets already for three hours.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:06.640
Like, just get out of my way.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:10:03.900
i remember one day um you you knew when the record was like you knew when the record was beatable because you would be halfway through your your your route and let's say you'd done it in 45 minutes and you knew that like if you just gave it a little extra energy that you could get through the day but i remember one day like okay i was halfway through my route and there was was this woman in front of me at this intersection, and the light turned yellow, and she like slowed down, and she really could have gone through the yellow light, and I could have like gone through, and it would have been perfect, and I, you know, had all the lights timed, and she slowed down, and I was sitting in my truck, and I lost it, and I lost it to the point where before the light turned green, I like let the truck, I like put it in neutral i was on a slight hill and i just sort of bumped her

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:58.280
and then immediately the minute i bumped her it's like you know it's like this huge guilt washed over me and i'm like okay that was like that was violent like okay it's one thing to be screaming and yelling in your truck listening to like you know motorhead it's another thing to physically violate somebody like that so then i realized okay you know i crossed some line that you know uh i guess you just shouldn't across what basically happens the psychology i think of a delivery driver is that the job is so boring this i mean and it's stressful there's no more stressful job than driving in traffic especially in an urban environment.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:04.160
And so then it all gets reduced to minutia.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:08.580
It gets reduced to this guy who's in this little car ahead of you who, like, somehow becomes your archenemy.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:18.960
And so it just creates an environment where you get very hostile and you just, you know, everybody is an inconvenience.

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:21.720
And all you want to do is get from point A to point B.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:37.850
Then there was the one part of the delivery job which was always, you know, there you are, you're screaming, you're yelling, or you're just like, you know, you're just having these outbursts.

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:41.620
And then there's the beautiful secretary walking to work with the short skirt.

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:55.520
And it would be like this, like all the noise would stop and you'd just have like this momentary like calm and then all of a sudden she would disappear from view and then you were like thrust right back into, you know, the craziness of traffic.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.200
and trying to get to where you're going.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:00.480
That would always save the day, actually.

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:11.920
Tony Starbucks was interviewed by Paul Tuff and Deirdre Dolan in New York.

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:42.260
the paper boy he comes each day our paper in his hand he throws the paper towards our door and on our fortune lands on sunny days on rainy days our paper boy comes by he never never misses us Because if he did, I'd cry.

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:46.080
Now, why don't we all try it together?

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:46.720
Ready?

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:48.900
One, two, three.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:52.060
The paper boy, he comes each day.

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:53.560
Act two, who you meet.

00:12:54.820 --> 00:13:06.120
There's the game that you play in your head when you work, and there are also the worlds that your job takes you into as you go house to house, office to office, like a spy, having little encounters with people along the way as a delivery person.

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:11.900
This short story is by Junot Diaz about guys who deliver gold crowns and bristles and schmalkies.

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:13.100
Pool tables.

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:18.040
A warning before we start that some of the language in this story might not be suitable for younger listeners.

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:23.900
The first time we try to deliver the gold crown, the lights are on in the house, but no one lets us in.

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:30.180
I bang on the front door, and Wayne hits the back, and I can hear our double drum shaking the windows.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.160
Right then, I have this feeling that someone is inside, laughing at us.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:38.420
This guy better have a good excuse, Wayne says.

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:40.040
This is bulls**t.

00:13:40.580 --> 00:13:44.680
You're telling me, I say, but Wayne's the one who takes his job too seriously.

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:48.420
He pounds some more on the door, his face jiggling.

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:52.840
A couple of times he raps on the windows, tries squinting through the curtains.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:55.680
I take a more philosophical approach.

00:13:56.580 --> 00:14:02.340
I walk over to the ditch that's been cut next to the road, a drainage pipe half-filled with water, and sit down.

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:10.000
I smoke and watch a mama duck and her three ducklings scavenge the grassy bank and then float downstream like they're on the same string.

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:13.700
Beautiful, I say, but Wayne doesn't hear.

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:16.380
He's banging on the door with a staple gun.

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:29.380
At nine, Wayne picks me up at the showroom, and by then I have our route planned out.

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:34.200
The order form tells me everything I need to know about the customers we'll be dealing with that day.

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:42.650
If someone is just getting a 52-inch card table delivered, then you know they aren't going to give you too much of a hassle, but they also aren't going to tip.

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:46.160
Those are your Spotswood, Sayreville, and Perth Amboy deliveries.

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:51.600
The pool tables go north to the rich suburbs, Livingston, Ridgewood, Bedminster.

00:14:53.480 --> 00:15:03.580
You should see our customers, doctors, diplomats, surgeons, presidents of universities, Ladies in slacks and silk tops who sport thin watches you could trade in for a car.

00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:09.900
Most of them prepare for us by laying down a path of yesterday's Washington Post from the front door to the game room.

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:12.060
I make them pick it all up.

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:15.100
I say, carajo, what if we slip?

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:18.220
Do you know what 200 pounds of slate could do to a floor?

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:21.980
The threat of property damage puts the chop-chop in their step.

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:22.180
step.

00:15:25.460 --> 00:15:30.790
Sometimes the customer has to jet to the store for cat food or a newspaper while we're in the middle of a job.

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:33.380
I'm sure you'll be all right, they say.

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:35.420
They never sound too sure.

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:38.960
Of course, I say, just show us where the silver's at.

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:51.240
The customer's ha-ha and we ha-ha, and then they agonize over leaving, linger by the front door, trying to memorize everything they own, as if if they don't know where to find us, who we work for.

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:02.540
Once they're gone, I don't have to worry about anyone bothering me.

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:10.020
I put down the ratchet, crack my knuckles, and explore, usually while Wayne is smoothing out the felt and doesn't need help.

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:13.860
I take cookies from the kitchen, razors from the bathroom cabinets.

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:16.660
Some of these houses have 20, 30 rooms.

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:21.260
On the ride back, I try to figure out how much loot it would take to fill up all that space.

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:31.320
I've been caught roaming around plenty of times, but you'd be surprised how quickly someone believes you're looking for the bathroom if you don't jump when you're discovered, if you just say hi.

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:35.460
After the paperwork's been signed, I have a decision to make.

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:40.120
If the customer has been good and tipped well, we call it even and leave.

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:46.080
If the customer has been an ass, maybe they yelled, maybe they let their kids throw golf balls at us.

00:16:46.230 --> 00:17:31.840
I ask for the bathroom Wayne will pretend that he hasn't seen this before excuse me, I'll say I let them show me the way to the bathroom usually I already know and once the door is shut I cram bubble bath drops into my pockets and throw fist-sized wads of toilet paper into the toilet I take a dump if I can and leave that for them most of the time Wayne and I work well together he's the driver and the money man and I do the lifting and handle the ass tonight we're on our way to Lawrenceville and he wants to talk to me about Charlene one of the showroom girls I haven't wanted to talk about women in months not since the a girlfriend.

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:34.800
I really want a piler, he tells me.

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:36.380
Maybe I'm one of the Madisons.

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:41.960
Man, I say, cutting my eyes towards him, don't you have a wife or something?

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:43.560
He gets quiet.

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:49.540
Twice this year, Wayne's cheated on his wife, and I've heard it all, the before and the after.

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:52.940
The last time his wife nearly tossed his ass out to the dogs.

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:55.460
Neither of the women seemed worth it to me.

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:57.780
One of them was even younger than Charlene.

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:01.400
Wayne can be a moody guy, and this is one of of those nights.

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:08.420
He slouches in the driver's seat and swerves through traffic, riding other people's bumpers like I've told him not to do.

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:18.400
I don't need a collision or a four-hour silent treatment, so I try to forget that I think his wife is good people and ask him if Charlene's given him any signals.

00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:20.840
He slows the truck down.

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:23.980
Signals like you wouldn't believe, he says.

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:40.600
The second time we bring the gold crown, the heavy curtain next to the door swings up like a Spanish fan.

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:44.780
A woman stares at me, and wanes too busy knocking to see.

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:46.720
Muñeca, I say.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:52.560
She's black and unsmiling, and then the curtain drops between us, a whisper on the glass.

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:57.840
She had on a T-shirt that said, No Problem, and didn't look like she owned the place.

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:04.700
She looked more like the help, and couldn't have been older than 20, and from the thinness of her face I pictured the rest of her skinny.

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:11.840
We stared at each other for a second at the most, not enough for me to notice the shape of her ears or if her lips were chapped.

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:14.020
I've fallen in love on less.

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:20.360
Later in the truck, on the way back to the showroom, Wayne mutters, This guy is dead.

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:21.520
I mean it.

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:32.220
The girlfriend calls sometimes, but not often.

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:37.100
She has found herself a new boyfriend, some sangano who works at a record store.

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:44.000
Dan is his name, and the way she says it, so painfully gringo, makes the corner of my eyes narrow.

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:47.420
The last time I saw her in person was in Hoboken.

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:55.040
She was with Dan, and hadn't yet told me about him, and hurried across the street in her high clogs to avoid me and my boys.

00:19:55.680 --> 00:20:24.380
a month before the sangano i went to her house a friend visiting a friend and her parents asked me how business was as if i balanced the books or something business is outstanding i said that's really wonderful to hear the father said you betcha he asked me to help him mow his lawn and while we were dribbling gas into the tank he offered me a job a real one that you can build on Utilities, he said, is nothing to be ashamed of.

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:38.140
The boss nearly kicked our asses over the gold crown.

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:43.560
The customer, an a**hole named Pruitt, called up crazy, said we were delinquent.

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:45.940
That's how the boss put it, delinquent.

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:50.680
We knew that's what the customer called us because the boss doesn't use words like that.

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:52.600
Look, boss, I said.

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:54.160
We knocked like crazy.

00:20:54.420 --> 00:20:57.700
I mean, we knocked like federal marshals, like Paul Bunyan.

00:20:58.200 --> 00:20:59.780
